---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { authors } from '../data/authors';

const siteUrl = 'https://chinalayoverguide.com';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About — China Layover Guide',
    description:
      'Who writes China Layover Guide, how the site earns its information, and what we can and cannot verify.',
    url: `${siteUrl}/about/`,
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'China Layover Guide',
      url: siteUrl,
    },
    about: authors.map((author) => ({
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: author.name,
      url: `${siteUrl}/authors/${author.slug}/`,
    })),
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | China Layover Guide"
  description="Who writes China Layover Guide, how we verify visa rules and payment details, and what we can and cannot check first-hand."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">About</h1>

    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600">
      China Layover Guide is for one traveler: the person with 10, 24, or 72 hours in China and no
      idea whether they can leave the airport. Most can, under the 240-hour visa-free transit
      policy, and a long layover can end up being the best part of a trip.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Who writes this</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        Two people, both living in Beijing. That matters, because a lot of what makes a China
        layover work only shows up on the ground: which payment app accepts a foreign card, whether
        the airport Wi-Fi will send you an SMS code, how long immigration actually takes.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        {
          authors.map((author) => (
            <a
              href={`/authors/${author.slug}/`}
              class="flex items-start gap-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5 transition hover:border-[var(--color-brand)]"
            >
              {author.avatar ? (
                <img
                  src={author.avatar}
                  alt={author.name}
                  width="56"
                  height="56"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  class="h-14 w-14 shrink-0 rounded-full object-cover"
                />
              ) : (
                <span
                  aria-hidden="true"
                  class="flex h-14 w-14 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-slate-100 text-base font-bold text-slate-600"
                >
                  {author.name.split(' ').map((p) => p[0]).join('')}
                </span>
              )}
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">{author.name}</h3>
                <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-brand)]">{author.role}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">{author.tagline}</p>
              </div>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How we verify things</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        Not everything on a travel site can be tested, and claiming otherwise is how travel writing
        goes wrong. Some details can be looked up and cited. Some can be checked by hand. We try to
        be specific about which is which.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Payment and eSIM guides: tested first-hand</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Bokang sets up each payment app and eSIM plan on a foreign card and checks what actually
            works before a guide is published. This is why those pages carry details like which
            card types fail at which step. Payment terms and plans change often, so they are
            re-tested and the pages updated.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Visa and transit rules: verified against sources</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Nobody can self-test a visa rule. Immigration policy is checked line by line against
            publications from the National Immigration Administration, plus the relevant airline or
            airport terms. Nora has used China's visa-free transit entry herself, which guides what
            the transit pages focus on, but the rules themselves are cited rather than tested.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Every factual page carries its evidence</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Each guide and city page shows a last-updated date and a list of sources at the foot of
            the page. If a rule changes, the page is revised and the date moves with it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What we do not claim</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-slate-600">
        <li>
          We are not immigration lawyers or a government body. Nothing here is legal advice, and the
          final decision on entry always belongs to the officer in front of you.
        </li>
        <li>
          We do not claim to have personally tested every airport, port or airline policy. Where we
          have not, the page cites the official source instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          We publish no sponsored rankings. Product mentions reflect what was tested, not who paid.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Corrections</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        Immigration and payment rules change constantly, and we would rather be corrected than be
        confidently wrong. If you spot an error, or a rule has changed, open an issue on the{' '}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/hbk671104/china-layover-guide/issues"
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-[var(--color-brand)] hover:underline"
        >
          project repository
        </a>
        . Every correction is checked against the source before the page is updated.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-slate-500">
      <a href="/authors/" class="text-[var(--color-brand)] hover:underline">
        Meet the authors &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
